    "- Split knowledge (no one holds the whole key) and dual control (two people must act together); key escrow and M of N control for recovery.",
    "- Codes (words or phrases substituted) versus ciphers (algorithms on characters or bits).",
    "- Boolean operations: AND, OR, NOT, XOR (exclusive OR is central to many ciphers); modulo arithmetic.",
    "## Historical and classical ciphers",
    "Caesar (substitution with shift), Vigenère (polyalphabetic), transposition (rearranging characters), running key, one-time pad (Vernam). The one-time pad is the only theoretically unbreakable cipher when the pad is truly random, at least as long as the message, used only once and kept secret; its key distribution is impractical. Frequency analysis breaks simple substitution ciphers.",
    "## Symmetric cryptography",
    "One shared secret key for encryption and decryption. Strengths: fast, efficient for bulk data, hardware-friendly. Weaknesses: secure key distribution, scalability (n(n-1)/2 keys for n users; 10 users need 45 keys), no nonrepudiation. Stream ciphers encrypt one bit or byte at a time (RC4, ChaCha20); block ciphers encrypt fixed-size blocks (AES, DES).",
    "## Symmetric algorithms",
    "- DES: 64-bit block, 56-bit key, 16 rounds; broken.",
    "- 3DES: three DES operations (EEE or EDE) with 2 or 3 keys; effective strength lower than key length; deprecated.",
    "- AES (Rijndael): 128-bit block; 128, 192 or 256-bit keys (10, 12, 14 rounds); the US government standard and the best answer for modern bulk encryption.",
    "- Blowfish (variable key up to 448 bits, 64-bit block), Twofish (AES finalist, 128-bit block), IDEA (128-bit key, used in early PGP), CAST, RC5/RC6, Skipjack (Clipper chip, escrow).",
