    "- CBC: each block XORed with previous ciphertext; needs an IV; errors propagate.",
    "- CFB and OFB: turn block ciphers into stream ciphers; OFB does not propagate errors.",
    "- CTR: counter mode, parallelizable, no error propagation.",
    "- GCM and CCM: authenticated encryption (confidentiality plus integrity); GCM is widely used in TLS 1.3.",
    "## Key management",
    "Covers generation (strong randomness), distribution (offline, public key encryption, Diffie-Hellman), storage (HSMs, TPMs, key vaults; never store keys with the data they protect), use, rotation, escrow and recovery, revocation and destruction. Cloud key management services and bring-your-own-key models let customers control keys; some compliance regimes require customer-managed keys.",
    "## Cryptographic lifecycle",
    "Algorithms and key lengths weaken over time as computing power grows. Organizations must inventory cryptography, retire weak algorithms (DES, 3DES, RC4, MD5, SHA-1), increase key sizes, and prepare for post-quantum cryptography (NIST selected ML-KEM/Kyber and ML-DSA/Dilithium; quantum computers running Shor's algorithm would break RSA and ECC, while Grover's algorithm halves symmetric strength, so AES-256 remains strong). Crypto agility makes swapping algorithms easier. FIPS 140-3 validates cryptographic modules."
