    "## Site and facility design",
    "Physical security protects people first, then facilities, equipment and information. Site selection considers crime rate, natural disaster risk (floods, earthquakes), proximity to emergency services, utilities, visibility and access. Facility design should involve security from the start.",
    "- CPTED (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design): natural access control (guiding people through doors, landscaping, bollards), natural surveillance (visibility, lighting, windows), territorial reinforcement (signs, fences, sense of ownership). Data centers should be inconspicuous and centrally located in the building, away from windows, water pipes and ground floors subject to flooding.",
    "- Functional order of controls: deter, deny, detect, delay, determine (assess) and decide (respond). Security controls can be administrative (site management, personnel, awareness, emergency procedures), technical (access control, intrusion detection, alarms, CCTV, HVAC monitoring, fire detection) and physical (fences, lighting, locks, guards, dogs).",
    "## Perimeter security",
    "- Fences: 3-4 feet deters casual trespassers; 6-7 feet is too high to climb easily; 8 feet or more with barbed or razor wire deters determined intruders. PIDAS fencing adds sensors.",
    "- Gates, turnstiles, bollards (block vehicles), barricades.",
    "- Lighting: deters and supports surveillance; standard is about 8 feet high with 2 foot-candles of illumination, lights spaced to avoid dark areas.",
    "- Security guards and dogs: guards provide judgment and flexibility but are costly and can be deceived; dogs are good for perimeter detection but raise liability.",
    "## Internal security",
    "- Mantraps (access control vestibules) prevent tailgating and piggybacking.",
    "- Locks: key, combination, electronic, smart locks; lock picking and bumping risks.",
