   "en": [
    "## Building an assessment and testing program",
    "Security testing verifies that controls work; assessments are broader reviews of a system or program; audits are formal evaluations by independent parties to demonstrate effectiveness to a third party. Testing should be risk-based, regularly scheduled and documented. Consider the speed, cost, risk to production and frequency of each test.",
    "- Internal audits are done by the organization's audit staff for internal use; external audits by independent firms (for boards, regulators, customers); third-party audits are conducted by or for another organization (vendor assessments, customers auditing suppliers).",
    "- Audit standards: COSO (internal control framework), COBIT (IT governance control objectives), ISO/IEC 27001 and 27002, SSAE 18 producing SOC reports.",
    "- SOC reports: SOC 1 (financial reporting controls), SOC 2 (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy; detailed and restricted distribution), SOC 3 (public summary). Type I covers design at a point in time; Type II covers operating effectiveness over a period (at least six months, more valuable).",
    "## Vulnerability assessments",
    "- Vulnerability scans: network discovery (nmap; port states open, closed, filtered; TCP SYN, connect, ACK, Xmas scans), network vulnerability scanning (Nessus, Qualys, OpenVAS), web application scanning, database scanning. Authenticated (credentialed) scans give deeper, more accurate results than unauthenticated scans.",
    "- Scan results include false positives (reported vulnerability that is not present) and false negatives (missed vulnerability, more dangerous).",
    "- Standards: SCAP components CVE (vulnerability naming), CVSS (severity scoring 0-10), CCE (configuration naming), CPE (product naming), XCCDF (checklists), OVAL (test language).",
    "- Vulnerability management workflow: detection, validation, remediation, verification; prioritize by severity and asset criticality.",
    "## Penetration testing",
    "- Phases (NIST SP 800-115): planning (rules of engagement, scope, written authorization: always required), information gathering and discovery, vulnerability scanning, exploitation, and reporting with remediation recommendations.",
    "- Knowledge levels: white box (full knowledge), gray box (partial), black box (zero knowledge, simulates external attacker).",
    "- Teams: red team (attackers), blue team (defenders), purple team (collaboration), white team (referees).",
    "- Breach and attack simulation tools automate testing; bug bounty programs reward outside researchers.",
    "## Testing software",
    "- Code review: peer review, Fagan inspection (formal: planning, overview, preparation, inspection, rework, follow-up). Static analysis (SAST, source code without running it) and dynamic analysis (DAST, running application).",
    "- Fuzz testing: mutation (dumb) fuzzing modifies existing input; generational (intelligent) fuzzing creates input from a data model. Finds crashes and input handling flaws.",
    "- Interface testing: APIs, user interfaces, physical interfaces. Misuse case testing: tests how an attacker could abuse the software. Test coverage analysis: tested use cases divided by total use cases (branch, condition, function, loop, statement coverage).",
    "- Website monitoring: passive (analyzes real traffic, real user monitoring) versus synthetic/active (scripted transactions).",
    "- Regression testing after changes; unit, integration, system and acceptance testing; positive, negative and boundary tests.",
    "## Training and exercises",
    "Tabletop exercises, simulations, and phishing tests evaluate people and plans. Breach and attack simulations validate controls continuously.",
    "## Security process data and KPIs",
    "Key metrics: account management reviews (sample accounts to confirm access is authorized), management review and approval, key performance and risk indicators (KPIs and KRIs: number of open vulnerabilities, time to resolve, time to detect, number of compromised accounts, repeat audit findings, user awareness training completion), backup verification (test restores, not just successful job logs), training and awareness, disaster recovery and business continuity testing. Log reviews and SIEM data support assessment.",
    "## Reporting and remediation",
    "Reports should match the audience (technical details for staff, risk-based summary for executives), describe findings with severity and evidence, and recommend remediation. Exceptions and compensating controls must be documented and approved by management. Ethical disclosure: report vulnerabilities to vendors responsibly. Track remediation to closure and retest."
   ],
