    "## Investigation types",
    "- Administrative (operational) investigations: internal matters such as policy violations or operational problems; lowest standard of evidence; root cause analysis for operational issues.",
    "- Criminal investigations: conducted by law enforcement for violations of criminal law; standard is beyond a reasonable doubt; strict evidence handling.",
    "- Civil investigations: disputes between parties; standard is preponderance of the evidence (more likely than not).",
    "- Regulatory investigations: by government agencies or industry bodies (such as PCI DSS assessments) for violations of regulations; standard varies.",
    "- Industry standards investigations: determine whether an organization meets standards such as PCI DSS.",
    "- eDiscovery: the legal process of collecting electronically stored information for litigation. The Electronic Discovery Reference Model (EDRM) stages: information governance, identification, preservation (legal hold), collection, processing, review, analysis, production, presentation. A legal hold requires preserving relevant data and suspends normal deletion.",
    "## Evidence",
    "- Admissible evidence must be relevant (helps determine a fact), material (related to the case) and competent (obtained legally).",
    "- Types: real evidence (physical objects, such as a hard drive; object evidence), documentary evidence (written items, including computer logs; subject to the best evidence rule, which prefers originals, and the parol evidence rule, which says a written agreement is assumed to contain all terms), testimonial evidence (witness statements; direct testimony versus expert opinion; hearsay is generally not admissible, but business records made in the normal course of business may be an exception).",
    "- Demonstrative evidence (charts, diagrams) helps explain other evidence.",
    "- Chain of custody (chain of evidence) documents who handled the evidence, when, where and why, from collection to court; evidence must be labeled, sealed and stored securely.",
    "- Artifacts and evidence collection: collect in order of volatility (CPU registers and cache, routing tables and ARP cache, RAM, temporary files, disk, remote logs, archival media). Take bit-by-bit images of drives, use write blockers, and compute hashes (SHA-256) to prove integrity; analyze copies, never the original.",
