    "## Software development concepts",
    "- Programming languages: machine language, assembly, compiled languages (C, Java bytecode), interpreted languages (Python, JavaScript); compiled code hides source but can be decompiled. Libraries and shared components bring third-party risk; maintain a software bill of materials (SBOM).",
    "- Object-oriented programming: objects, classes, methods, messages, inheritance, polymorphism, encapsulation, abstraction. Good design has high cohesion (a module does one thing) and low coupling (modules are independent).",
    "- Assurance: confidence that security is built in throughout the lifecycle. Avoiding and mitigating system failure: input validation (allow lists better than block lists), authentication and session management, error handling (generic messages to users, details in logs), logging, fail-secure versus fail-open design.",
    "## Systems development lifecycle (SDLC)",
    "Phases: conceptual definition, functional requirements determination, control specifications development (security requirements), design review, coding, code review walk-through, user acceptance testing, maintenance and change management. Security must be included from the beginning (security by design), which is far cheaper than adding it later. Certification (technical evaluation) and accreditation (management's formal acceptance of risk, authorization to operate) follow.",
    "## Lifecycle and development models",
    "- Waterfall: sequential phases, can only return to previous phase; modified waterfall adds verification and validation. Spiral model: iterations, each a mini waterfall with risk assessment (prototypes).",
    "- Agile (Agile Manifesto: individuals and interactions, working software, customer collaboration, responding to change); Scrum (sprints, product owner, scrum master, daily standups), Kanban, XP. Rapid application development (RAD).",
    "- Maturity models: Software Capability Maturity Model (SW-CMM) and CMMI levels: 1 initial, 2 repeatable, 3 defined, 4 managed, 5 optimizing. IDEAL model: initiating, diagnosing, establishing, acting, learning. SAMM (OWASP Software Assurance Maturity Model) focuses on security: governance, design, implementation, verification, operations.",
    "- Gantt charts and PERT (program evaluation review technique) for scheduling.",
    "- Change and configuration management: request control, change control, release control. DevOps integrates development, quality assurance and operations; DevSecOps adds security, shifting it left; CI/CD pipelines automate builds, tests and deployment with security scanning.",
    "## APIs, testing and code repositories",
    "- Application programming interfaces must enforce authentication (API keys, OAuth), authorization, rate limiting and input validation. Avoid exposing sensitive data.",
    "- Software testing: unit, integration, system, acceptance, regression; white box versus black box; static (SAST) and dynamic (DAST), interactive (IAST), runtime application self-protection (RASP), fuzzing, software composition analysis for third-party components.",
    "- Code repositories (GitHub, GitLab): protect access, never commit secrets (API keys), review changes, sign commits.",
    "- Service-level agreements for software and cloud services; acquired software (COTS, open source, SaaS) must be assessed for security (vendor assessments, contractual requirements, code escrow).",
    "## Databases and data storage",
    "- Database management systems: hierarchical, network, relational (most common; tables or relations, rows or tuples, columns or attributes; cardinality is the number of rows, degree the number of columns). Keys: candidate, primary (unique row identifier), alternate, foreign (enforces referential integrity). SQL: DDL (define structure) and DML (manipulate data); NoSQL databases (key-value, document, graph).",
    "- Transactions follow ACID: atomicity (all or nothing), consistency (database rules respected), isolation (transactions do not interfere), durability (committed transactions persist). Use commit and rollback, two-phase commit for distributed databases.",
    "- Security for multilevel databases: concurrency control (locking prevents lost updates and dirty reads), aggregation and inference (see chapter 9), polyinstantiation, views, data mining and data warehouses (protect as highly sensitive), ODBC.",
    "- Storage threats: illegitimate access, covert channels, unauthorized modification.",
    "## Expert systems and machine learning",
    "Expert systems use a knowledge base (if-then rules) and an inference engine. Machine learning learns from data (supervised and unsupervised); neural networks underpin deep learning. Risks include data poisoning, model theft and adversarial inputs."
